ce:section.ce:para:
The diagram (Fig. 1
) shows the possibility of C3S formation at temperatures in the range 1250–2150 °C. Typically it will form by combination of C2S and CaO when the temperature rises above 1250 °C. In a pure system at room temperature, C3S is metastable and has a triclinic structure. At rising temperature polymorphs show first a monoclinic and later rhombohedral structure. Accurate determination of the transition temperatures is difficult, with different methods like X-ray diffraction and optical microscopy yielding slightly different results ([7] p. 111). Commonly agreed data on transition temperatures are given in Table 1
.
